<div class="shell">

<aside>
  <div class="panel">
    <div class="panel-head"><span class="eyebrow">About you</span></div>
    <div class="panel-body">

      <div class="field">
        <label for="prov">Where you live</label>
        <div class="seg-ctl" id="prov">
          <button type="button" data-v="BC" aria-pressed="true">British&nbsp;Columbia</button>
          <button type="button" data-v="soon" aria-pressed="false" disabled title="Coming soon">Other&nbsp;— soon</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="field">
        <label>Your household</label>
        <div class="seg-ctl" id="marital">
          <button type="button" data-v="single" aria-pressed="true">Single</button>
          <button type="button" data-v="couple" aria-pressed="false">Married or common-law</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="field">
        <label>Children under 18</label>
        <div class="seg-ctl" id="haskids">
          <button type="button" data-v="no" aria-pressed="true">No children</button>
          <button type="button" data-v="yes" aria-pressed="false">I have children</button>
        </div>
        <div id="kidsWrap" hidden>
          <div class="kids" id="kids"></div>
          <div class="hint">Under 6 carries a larger benefit than 6–17.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="divider"></div>

      <div class="field">
        <label for="income">Your income before tax</label>
        <input type="number" id="income" value="68000" min="0" max="500000" step="1000">
        <input type="range" id="incomeRange" value="68000" min="15000" max="250000" step="500" aria-label="Income">
      </div>

      <div class="field" id="partnerField" hidden>
        <label for="partner">Your partner's income before tax</label>
        <input type="number" id="partner" value="55000" min="0" max="500000" step="1000">
      </div>

      <div class="field">
        <label for="save">What you can save each month</label>
        <input type="number" id="save" value="500" min="0" max="20000" step="50">
        <div class="hint">Even small amounts matter far more than most people expect.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="field">
        <label for="have">What you've already saved or invested</label>
        <input type="number" id="have" value="10000" min="0" max="5000000" step="1000">
      </div>

      <div class="divider"></div>

      <div class="field">
        <label>Your age, and when you want to stop working</label>
        <div class="row">
          <input type="number" id="age" value="32" min="16" max="80" step="1" aria-label="Current age">
          <span class="hint" style="margin:0;white-space:nowrap">→ retire at</span>
          <input type="number" id="retage" value="65" min="45" max="75" step="1" aria-label="Retirement age">
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="field">
        <label for="target">Yearly income you'd want in retirement</label>
        <input type="number" id="target" value="50000" min="0" max="300000" step="1000">
        <div class="hint">In today's money. Many people aim for roughly 60–70% of what they earn now.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="field">
        <label for="growth">Yearly investment growth you assume</label>
        <div class="row">
          <input type="range" id="growth" value="8" min="0" max="12" step="0.5" style="flex:1">
          <span class="num" id="growthOut" style="width:52px;text-align:right;font-weight:600">8.0%</span>
        </div>
        <div class="hint" id="realHint"></div>
      </div>

      <div class="field">
        <label class="row" style="gap:8px;cursor:pointer;font-size:13px">
          <input type="checkbox" id="fhsa" checked style="accent-color:var(--stat)">
          <span>I could buy a first home someday</span>
        </label>
        <div class="hint">Unlocks the FHSA — the single best account most Canadians have never opened.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="field">
        <label for="matchRate">Employer retirement match, if any</label>
        <div class="row">
          <input type="number" id="matchRate" value="0" min="0" max="200" step="5" aria-label="Match cents">
          <span class="hint" style="margin:0;white-space:nowrap">¢ per $1, up to</span>
          <input type="number" id="matchCap" value="0" min="0" max="50000" step="500" aria-label="Match cap">
        </div>
        <div class="hint">Check your benefits handbook. This is free money and it is routinely left unclaimed.</div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </div>
</aside>

<main>

  <!-- HERO: growth first. Tax comes later. -->
  <div class="panel">
    <div class="hero">
      <div class="eyebrow" id="heroLabel">What you'd have when you retire</div>
      <div class="hero-num" id="heroNum">$0</div>
      <div class="hero-sub" id="heroSub"></div>
      <div class="split" role="img" id="splitBar">
        <div class="sp-you" id="spYou"></div>
        <div class="sp-grow" id="spGrow"></div>
        <div class="sp-gov" id="spGov"></div>
      </div>
      <div class="splitkey">
        <span class="spk"><i style="background:var(--stat)"></i><span><b id="kYou">$0</b>you put in</span></span>
        <span class="spk"><i style="background:var(--keep)"></i><span><b id="kGrow">$0</b>growth on top</span></span>
        <span class="spk"><i style="background:var(--gold)"></i><span><b id="kGov">$0</b>government help, invested</span></span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <details>
      <summary>Why is this smaller than other calculators show me?</summary>
      <div class="panel-body">
        Because this is in <b>today's money</b>. A calculator that promises you a
        seven-figure balance in forty years is not lying, but that number will not buy
        what it sounds like — inflation quietly eats most of it. Everything here is
        adjusted so you can compare it directly to what things cost now.
      </div>
    </details>
  </div>

  <!-- GROWTH CHART -->
  <div class="panel">
    <div class="panel-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">How it grows over time</span>
      <span class="pills" id="horizons"></span>
    </div>
    <div class="chartwrap"><svg id="growthChart" viewBox="0 0 760 300" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"></svg></div>
    <div class="readout" id="growthRead"></div>
    <details>
      <summary>The part almost nobody is told</summary>
      <div class="panel-body" id="crossoverText"></div>
    </details>
  </div>

  <!-- RETIREMENT TRACK -->
  <div class="panel">
    <div class="panel-head"><span class="eyebrow">Are you on track?</span></div>
    <div class="track">
      <div class="meter" id="meter">
        <i class="m-gov" id="mGov"></i><i class="m-port" id="mPort"></i>
        <span class="target" id="mTarget"></span>
      </div>
      <div class="trackrow">
        <span>Government pensions (CPP + OAS) <b id="tGov">$0</b></span>
        <span>From your investments <b id="tPort">$0</b></span>
        <span>Your target <b id="tTarget">$0</b></span>
      </div>
      <div class="verdict" id="verdict"></div>
    </div>
    <details>
      <summary>What are CPP and OAS, and why do they matter here?</summary>
      <div class="panel-body">
        <dl>
          <dt>CPP — Canada Pension Plan</dt>
          <dd>A pension you build up by working. The average person starting at 65 gets about
          <b>$925 a month</b>, not the $1,508 maximum you'll see quoted — that maximum assumes
          almost 40 years of contributions at the highest income level. We use the average, because
          it's closer to most people.</dd>
          <dt>OAS — Old Age Security</dt>
          <dd>Paid from age 65 based on how long you've lived in Canada, not on whether you worked.
          Roughly <b>$742 a month</b> at full residency.</dd>
          <dt>Why it changes the answer</dt>
          <dd>These cover the first slice of your retirement income, so your savings only need to
          cover the rest. Calculators that ignore them make the target look terrifying and put
          people off starting at all.</dd>
        </dl>
      </div>
    </details>
  </div>

  <!-- ALLOCATION -->
  <div class="panel">
    <div class="panel-head"><span class="eyebrow">Where to put your money, in order</span></div>
    <div id="alloc"></div>
    <div class="total">
      <span class="k">What the government gives back in year one</span>
      <span><span class="v" id="totalVal">$0</span> <span class="k" id="totalPct"></span></span>
    </div>
    <div class="bd" id="breakdown"></div>
    <div id="warnings"></div>
    <details>
      <summary>Plain-English guide to these accounts</summary>
      <div class="panel-body">
        <dl>
          <dt>TFSA — Tax-Free Savings Account</dt>
          <dd>Money goes in after tax. Everything it earns is yours, forever, tax-free. You can take
          it out any time for any reason. It is not a savings account — you can hold investments in it,
          and most people should.</dd>
          <dt>RRSP — Registered Retirement Savings Plan</dt>
          <dd>You get a tax refund for putting money in, then pay tax when you take it out in
          retirement. It works when you earn more now than you will later.</dd>
          <dt>FHSA — First Home Savings Account</dt>
          <dd>The best of both: a refund going in, <em>and</em> tax-free coming out if you buy a first
          home. $8,000 a year, $40,000 lifetime. Introduced in 2023, and badly under-used.</dd>
          <dt>Employer match</dt>
          <dd>If your employer adds money when you contribute, that is an instant return no
          investment can match. Always take all of it first.</dd>
        </dl>
      </div>
    </details>
  </div>

  <!-- COST OF WAITING -->
  <div class="panel">
    <div class="panel-head"><span class="eyebrow">The cost of waiting</span></div>
    <div class="panel-body" id="waitBox"></div>
  </div>

  <!-- MARGINAL RATE -->
  <div class="panel">
    <div class="panel-head"><span class="eyebrow">Why saving is worth more than you think</span></div>
    <div class="panel-body">
      <div style="font-size:13.5px;color:var(--muted);margin-bottom:14px" id="dollarIntro"></div>
      <div class="dollar" role="img" id="dollarBar">
        <div class="d-tax" id="dTax"></div><div class="d-claw" id="dClaw"></div><div class="d-keep" id="dKeep"></div>
      </div>
      <div class="splitkey">
        <span class="spk"><i style="background:var(--stat)"></i><span><b id="cTax">0¢</b>income tax</span></span>
        <span class="spk"><i style="background:var(--claw)"></i><span><b id="cClaw">0¢</b>lost child benefit</span></span>
        <span class="spk"><i style="background:var(--keep)"></i><span><b id="cKeep">0¢</b>you keep</span></span>
      </div>
      <div style="margin-top:14px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.55" id="dollarWhy"></div>
    </div>
  </div>

</main>
</div>

<footer>
  Federal brackets: CRA 2026. British Columbia brackets: Province of B.C. Canada Child Benefit: 2026–27 benefit year.
  CPP and OAS: Canada.ca, January 2026 — average, not maximum. Growth figures are shown in today's dollars, adjusted for 2% inflation.
  Everything runs in your browser; nothing you type is sent anywhere or stored.
  <br><br>
  This is an educational model, not tax, financial, or investment advice. Investment returns are not guaranteed and past performance does not predict future results.
  It does not account for every credit, deduction, or personal circumstance. Confirm your contribution room in CRA My Account, and consider speaking to a qualified professional before acting.
</footer>
